## C. Robustness
- **Selection (po<=0.05, n=79)**: order unchanged — mean d Cognitive 0.301 / Economics 0.268 / Social 0.240; median |rr|/|ro| 0.671 / 0.698 / 0.220; Wilcoxon p<=1.7e-06 / 1.3e-03.
- **abs-difference** |fiso|-|fisr| identical to signed d (ro>0 in all 86 rows; 0 sign disagreements).
- **relative_es**: recomputed rr/ro differs from the published column in exactly one row — `B Eitam, RR Hassin, Y Schul` (published -0.472 vs rr/ro +0.472). Dropping it: Social median rr/ro 0.131 -> 0.164, mean 0.278 -> 0.301; conclusions unchanged.
- **Weight sensitivity** (weighted mean d): Cognitive 0.186-0.243, Social 0.186-0.257 across all weighting schemes; Economics 0.013-0.050 under any N-based weight vs 0.266 unweighted.
- **Protocol confound**: d ~ domain + ro + log(no) + log(nr): domain coefficients 0.038-0.079 (p>=0.35), R2=0.12 — no domain gap in absolute d either way.
- **Ratio stability**: restricting to ro>=0.30 keeps the retention gap (MW Cognitive vs Social p=0.019; Social vs Economics p=0.031). Restricting to sign-preserving pairs (rr>0) attenuates it (MW p=0.285) but the log-model still holds (Social beta -0.84, p=0.0095).

## D. Why precision-weighting collapses Economics
- Weight share in ee: top-1 pair = 43% of total ee weight (Cognitive 16%, Social 7%).
- That pair: `de Clippel et al. (AER 2014)`, no=790, nr=780, ro=0.118, rr=0.267 -> d = **-0.155**, rr/ro=2.26, replicated=1, i.e. the replication is *larger* than the original.
- ee weighted mean d: +0.013 -> +0.141 (drop that pair) -> +0.147 (drop 2) -> +0.259 (drop 6); unweighted rises 0.266 -> 0.381.
- Not purely one point: within-ee Spearman rho(d, weight) = -0.60 (p=.008); -0.53 (p=.029) after dropping the top pair, i.e. larger-N ee pairs systematically show less shrinkage. In Cognitive/Social weighting is nearly neutral (drop-top-1: 0.206 -> 0.200; 0.208 -> 0.211).

